WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.253
Hi, I'm Banana!

00:00:02.253 --> 00:00:05.786
Chinchilla's scaling law adds a size term to a data term.

00:00:05.786 --> 00:00:08.998
Here's why that plus sign matters, and what replaces it.

00:00:10.725 --> 00:00:14.911
The idea: a sum forces the size-data interaction to exactly zero.

00:00:14.911 --> 00:00:19.859
Skaling raises the sum to one free exponent, k, so they can interact.

00:00:21.567 --> 00:00:22.678
One term at a time.

00:00:23.567 --> 00:00:26.812
A over N to the alpha: model size, as in Chinchilla.

00:00:27.232 --> 00:00:30.357
B over D to the beta: data, with its own exponent.

00:00:30.777 --> 00:00:32.216
Raise the sum to k.

00:00:32.216 --> 00:00:33.368
At one, it's Chinchilla.

00:00:33.368 --> 00:00:35.958
Fitted on Farseer, k is zero point four one.

00:00:36.378 --> 00:00:38.971
E is the floor, which these runs can't pin down.

00:00:40.775 --> 00:00:44.633
Add two terms and the cross-derivative is zero by construction, which leaves

00:00:44.633 --> 00:00:47.205
a saddle of errors at the grid's corners.

00:00:47.625 --> 00:00:51.234
With k below one it turns negative: bigger models extract more

00:00:51.234 --> 00:00:52.547
from the same token.

00:00:54.667 --> 00:00:57.593
A near-perfect fit inside the grid proves little.

00:00:57.593 --> 00:00:59.788
Scaling laws are used to extrapolate.

00:01:00.208 --> 00:01:04.260
So train only the cheap edges: small models across many data budgets,

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:06.847
and small data budgets across many sizes.

00:01:06.847 --> 00:01:08.704
About a tenth of the compute.

00:01:10.092 --> 00:01:14.213
Fitted on that L, Skaling beats Chinchilla fitted on everything: one point

00:01:14.213 --> 00:01:17.991
five one percent error far out, against two point four six.

00:01:19.675 --> 00:01:22.049
And the budget number flips sign.

00:01:22.049 --> 00:01:26.006
Model-free estimates say optimal tokens per parameter fall with compute.

00:01:26.006 --> 00:01:26.798
Skaling agrees.

00:01:26.798 --> 00:01:28.380
Chinchilla says they rise.

00:01:29.933 --> 00:01:31.148
Size and data interact.

00:01:31.148 --> 00:01:34.489
One exponent lets the law say so, and that moves where

00:01:34.489 --> 00:01:35.704
the compute should go.

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:41.817
To recap: one plus sign, one exponent, and a cheap L-shaped grid

00:01:41.817 --> 00:01:42.956
to fit it on.

00:01:43.376 --> 00:01:45.512
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:45.512 --> 00:01:46.122
I'm Banana.

00:01:46.122 --> 00:01:46.427
Bye!
